3.8. Larval and Adult Density
It is important to ensure a consistent number of larvae per container depending on the amount of feed. The amount of feed one larva could consume varies between developmental stages (Figure 1b–f). The larvae have to be provided with enough feed to minimize intraspecific competition. However, too many larvae in a container can increase the substrate temperature to an uncomfortable level, which might result in larval escape and lower larval weight gain. The number of larvae used per unit area, and their stages as well as the container dimensions differ greatly between studies (Table 4). The lack of standardization makes the comparability of data considerably difficult.
